# Phase 15b Backend Configuration
# Copy to .env.local for local development, or set these in the Vercel
# dashboard for production. NEVER commit .env.local (it is gitignored).

# ============================================================================
# Google OAuth2 — server-side (api/auth/*)
# ============================================================================
# Get these from https://console.cloud.google.com/
#
# Steps:
# 1. Create a Google Cloud project
# 2. Enable the Google Drive API
# 3. Configure the OAuth consent screen (Audience → External), and add
#    yourself as a test user so you can sign in before publishing
# 4. Under Data Access, add the scope
#    https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.appdata — the app's own hidden
#    folder, nothing else. It is a NON-SENSITIVE scope, so publishing the
#    app needs only basic verification, with no security assessment.
# 5. PUBLISH the app. This is not optional: Google issues a 7-DAY refresh
#    token while the consent screen is in "Testing", because drive.appdata
#    is outside the name/email/profile set that would exempt us. Left in
#    Testing, sync works for about a week and then silently stops.
# 6. Create OAuth 2.0 credentials of type "Web application"
# 7. Add Authorized JavaScript origins — note: ORIGINS, not redirect URIs.
#    GIS popup mode uses the calling page's origin, so there is no redirect
#    path to register. Enter the origin only (scheme + host), with NO
#    trailing slash — a slash never matches, and the resulting failure
#    reads like an app bug rather than a console typo:
#      - http://localhost:5173   (Vite dev server)
#      - https://trackz-sable.vercel.app
# 8. Copy the Client ID and Client Secret below

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id-here.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-here

# NOTE: there is deliberately no GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI here.
# In GIS popup mode, redirect_uri must equal the origin of the page that
# called initCodeClient, so it is derived per-request from the Origin/Host
# headers (see callerOrigin in lib/apiAuth.ts). A hardcoded value would
# fail on every deployment whose origin differed from it — notably every
# Vercel preview URL.

# NOTE: there is also no CORS_ORIGIN.
# The browser always reaches this API same-origin: shared domain in
# production, and a Vite proxy in dev (see trackz-app/vite.config.ts).
# Issuing no Access-Control-Allow-Origin at all is part of the CSRF
# defense for these cookie-authenticated endpoints.

# ============================================================================
# Google OAuth2 — client-side (trackz-app)
# ============================================================================
# The SAME client ID as above, exposed to the frontend bundle. Vite only
# inlines variables prefixed with VITE_, and only this public half of the
# credential pair may ever be sent to the browser.
#
# This belongs in trackz-app/.env.local (Vite reads its own directory),
# not in this root file.
#
# VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id-here.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ============================================================================
# Security notes
# ============================================================================
# - GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET must never appear in frontend code or a VITE_ var
# - The refresh token is never sent to the browser as JSON; it is stored in
#   an httpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Secure cookie (see lib/apiAuth.ts)
# - Only the ~1h access token reaches JavaScript, held in memory only
# - Never log tokens
# - Rotate the client secret if it is ever exposed

# ============================================================================
# Running locally
# ============================================================================
# Two processes, because the API needs the Vercel runtime:
#   vercel dev --listen 3000     # serves /api/auth/*
#   cd trackz-app && npm run dev # serves the app on :5173, proxies /api
#
# Always use http://localhost:5173 in the browser. Hitting :3000 directly
# would make the app and API different origins, and a SameSite=Lax cookie
# is not sent cross-site — session restore would break.
